---
// Downloads: the scores and the history as CSV and JSON, with the licence.
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { scoreRows, historyRows, priceRows, panelRows, PANEL_FROM } from "../../lib/exports.js";
import { lastUpdated, formatDate, DATA_SNAPSHOT, ESTIMATE_WINDOW } from "../../lib/countries.js";
import { archivedCount } from "../../lib/archive.js";
import { SHOW_PRICES } from "../../lib/config.js";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const repo = "https://github.com/burningtree/cannabis-freedom-index";
const files = [
  { name: "scores", title: "Scores", rows: scoreRows, about: "One row per country, disputed territory or dependency (Crown Dependencies and overseas territories, with dependency_of naming the country that answers for them): index score, the six segment scores, rank, tier, legal status, seed and clone status where researched, population and estimated cannabis users." },
  { name: "panel", title: "Scores by year", rows: panelRows, about: `One row per place and year from ${PANEL_FROM} to ${DATA_SNAPSHOT}: the index score and the six segment scores as they stood at the end of each year, rebuilt from the history. Made for joining with other country-year data.` },
  { name: "history", title: "History", rows: historyRows, about: "One row per history entry: date, country, category, title, description, the score before and after, how far it has been checked, and its sources." },
  { name: "prices", title: "Flower prices", rows: priceRows, about: "One row per sourced flower-price observation: country, date, street or medical market, local price and US dollars per gram." },
].filter((f) => SHOW_PRICES || f.name !== "prices");
---
<Base title="Data and licence" description={`Download Cannabis Freedom Index scores${SHOW_PRICES ? ", history, and cannabis prices" : " and history"} as CSV or JSON. Data is CC BY 4.0, code is MIT.`}>
  <main class="changes-page">
    <section class="hero hero-sm">
      <p class="eyebrow">Data</p>
      <h1>Download the data</h1>
      <p class="lede">Everything on this site is built from open files. Take the scores{SHOW_PRICES ? ", history and retail prices" : " and history"} as a spreadsheet or as JSON, or read the source files on GitHub. Last updated {formatDate(lastUpdated)}.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="downloads">
      {files.map((f) => (
        <section class="card download">
          <h2>{f.title}</h2>
          <p>{f.about}</p>
          <p class="download-meta">{f.rows.length} rows · {Object.keys(f.rows[0]).length} columns</p>
          <p class="page-links">
            <a class="btn" href={`${base}data/${f.name}.csv`} download>{f.name}.csv</a>
            <a class="btn" href={`${base}data/${f.name}.json`} download>{f.name}.json</a>
          </p>
          <details>
            <summary>Columns</summary>
            <p class="download-cols">{Object.keys(f.rows[0]).join(", ")}</p>
          </details>
        </section>
      ))}
    </div>

    <section class="prose" id="panel">
      <h2>Reading the yearly scores</h2>
      <p>The file <code>panel</code> turns the history into a table researchers can use directly: a score for every place in every year. Each score is the one in force on 31 December of that year. Segment scores run from 0 to 10; <code>score</code> is the index from 0 to 100, weighted as in the methodology.</p>
      <p>The column <code>basis</code> says what a row rests on, and should be used to filter:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>recorded</code>: the scores follow from history entries with a known date.</li>
        <li><code>interpolated</code>: no first law is recorded for the place, so its first restriction is assumed to have come between {ESTIMATE_WINDOW[0]} and {ESTIMATE_WINDOW[1]}, and the scores move in a straight line across those years. This is an assumption, not a finding.</li>
        <li><code>baseline</code>: years before the place's first recorded change. The scores are its starting point, usually no cannabis-specific rules at all.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Scores before each change are editorial estimates in every case. Places are identified by a two-letter code (<code>id</code>) and by the ISO 3166-1 numeric code (<code>iso_numeric</code>), which is empty for disputed territories. Disputed territories and dependencies are scored but not ranked, and are left out of every average. Borders are today's: a country that was part of another state carries the law that applied on its territory.</p>
      <h2>How to cite</h2>
      <p>Cannabis Freedom Index, {DATA_SNAPSHOT} edition, cannabisfreedom.fyi, retrieved on the date of download. The data changes as laws do, so keep the file you used; the <a href={`${base}changelog/`}>changelog</a> lists every score change since.</p>
      <h2>Sources that last</h2>
      <p>Links break. Where a copy of a web source has been saved in the Internet Archive, the history file gives its address in <code>source_archive_urls</code>, and each history entry links to it beside the original. Books and chapters are preserved as structured bibliographic citations in <code>source_citations</code>, including authors, editors, publisher, year, ISBN and page locator where supplied. {archivedCount} web sources have a recorded copy so far.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose" id="licence">
      <h2>Licence</h2>
      <p><strong>The data</strong> (scores, explanations{SHOW_PRICES ? ", history and prices" : " and history"}) is published under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Creative Commons Attribution 4.0</a>. You may copy, adapt and republish it, including commercially, as long as you credit "Cannabis Freedom Index" and link to this site.</p>
      <p><strong>The code</strong> is under the <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/LICENSE`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MIT licence</a>.</p>
      <p><strong>Data from others</strong> keeps its own terms: population figures come from the World Bank and Our World in Data (both CC BY 4.0), cannabis-use rates from the UN's World Drug Report as collected on Wikipedia, and map shapes from Natural Earth (public domain). The outside datasets shown beside the index, such as Pew Research Center's survey and the Human Freedom Index, belong to their publishers; their files and sources are listed on the <a href={`${base}sources/#datasets`}>sources page</a>.</p>
      <p>Scores are editorial estimates and many entries rest on secondary sources. This is not legal advice.</p>
      <h2>Stay up to date</h2>
      <p>The <a href={`${base}changelog/`}>changelog</a> lists every score change, and the <a href={`${base}rss.xml`}>RSS feed</a> carries the latest fifty. The source files, one per country, are on <a href={repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>.</p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
